{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00405 - MONDO_6004",
  "verbalization": "Dexbrompheniramine, a histamine H1 receptor antagonist, is implicated in the pathogenesis of Vasomotor Rhinitis through its role in modulating histamine signaling. The evidence suggests that Dexbrompheniramine may be repurposed for Vasomotor Rhinitis, as it is linked to Urticaria, a condition that shares common pathophysiological mechanisms with Vasomotor Rhinitis, such as mast cell activation and histamine release. The presence of multiple indirect pathways—through Rupatadine, Diphenylpyraline, and Pheniramine—indicates a potential mechanistic link, though the exact mode of action remains unclear. These pathways are all histamine receptor modulators, suggesting a shared mechanism of action in the regulation of inflammatory responses. The integration of these pathways supports the hypothesis that Dexbrompheniramine may exert a therapeutic effect on Vasomotor Rhinitis by targeting histamine signaling, though further experimental validation is required to confirm this relationship.",
